# Jupyter-deploy: Terraform AWS EKS OIDC template

## Project organization

This project manages the AWS resources to deploy JupyterLab workspaces on an Amazon EKS cluster.
It uses Terraform as the infrastructure-as-code engine, provisions an EKS cluster with the
jupyter-k8s operator for workspace lifecycle management, and controls access with GitHub identities via Dex.

**IMPORTANT:** An Amazon Route53 hosted zone for the domain MUST exist in the AWS account.

The template deploys:
- A VPC with public and private subnets across 2 AZs
- An EKS cluster in the VPC with Identity provider configured for GitHub
- One EKS managed node group, `platform`, labelled `jupyter-deploy/role=platform` and untainted. It
  runs the operators and platform pods, and the cluster-autoscaler scales it within
  `platform_min_size`/`platform_max_size`.
- Karpenter NodePools for everything else, each labelled AND tainted `jupyter-deploy/role=<role>`
  so only pods carrying the matching toleration land there: `routing` for the ingress/auth tier,
  plus one pool per entry in `workspace_nodepools` (default `workspace-cpu`, role `workspaces`) and
  an optional GPU pool. Use `jd pool list` to see them.
- EKS add-ons: vpc-cni, kube-proxy, coredns, pod-identity-agent, ebs-csi-driver, cert-manager, external-dns
- A public ACM certificate for `<subdomain>.<domain>`, DNS-validated against the hosted zone
  (`engine/acm.tf`, output `acm_certificate_arn`). Public TLS terminates at the NLB with this
  certificate and is re-encrypted to Traefik, which serves a certificate from a private CA that
  cert-manager mints in the cluster: there is no Let's Encrypt and no ACME challenge, so nothing
  here needs an email address. Note the NLB does NOT verify what Traefik presents, so a stalled
  in-cluster certificate is invisible at the edge — check `kubectl get certificate -n <router-namespace>`.
- Helm releases: traefik-crds, jupyter-k8s operator, jupyter-k8s-aws-oidc (OIDC routing + web UI),
  karpenter, keda and prometheus (which KEDA scales the routing tier from), cluster-autoscaler,
  karpenter-nodepools / workspace-defaults / github-rbac (local charts), optionally
  nvidia-device-plugin when a GPU pool is enabled, and optionally aws-for-fluent-bit to send pod
  logs to CloudWatch.
- Dex as an OIDC identity provider on the cluster for kubectl authentication

Unlike the single-host EC2 template, this is a multi-app deployment: each user gets their own
workspace (a "server"), addressed by `--name` and `--scope` (the Kubernetes namespace).

