### Manage access with a GitHub organization and teams

Access to workspaces is controlled by GitHub team membership, configured through template
variables (this template does not use the `jd users`/`jd teams` commands).

- `oauth_allowed_teams` — list of GitHub teams allowed to sign in, each in `org:team` format
  (e.g. `my-org:data-science`). Members of these teams can authenticate via the browser.
- `workspace_namespaces` — the Kubernetes namespaces where workspaces run, and the teams granted
  each. Empty (the default): every team in `oauth_allowed_teams` may create workspaces in
  `default`. Non-empty: each entry `{name, teams, templates}` creates a namespace (or adopts
  `default`) whose Role and RoleBinding cover only its listed teams; no namespace grants every
  team, so list `default` explicitly to keep using it. The first entry is the default namespace
  of the web app and of `jd server`; `jd component`/`jd health` read the `jupyterlab` template
  from `default` when listed, else from the first entry. `templates` adds `workspace_templates`
  configs to the built-in `jupyterlab` card in that namespace; `bedrock: "false"` withholds
  Bedrock credentials.

A team must be in both: `jd config` refuses a namespace team missing from `oauth_allowed_teams`,
and warns about an allowed team no namespace lists (its members sign in but cannot create workspaces).

```bash
# allow two GitHub teams (pass each entry as a separate flag)
jd config --oauth-allowed-teams my-org:data-science --oauth-allowed-teams my-org:ml-platform
jd up
```

To isolate teams, edit `overrides:` in `variables.yaml`, then run `jd config` and `jd up`:

```yaml
overrides:
  workspace_namespaces:
    - name: default
      teams: my-org:data-science,my-org:ml-platform
    - name: ml-platform
      teams: my-org:ml-platform
      templates: jupyterlab-gpu
```

When `jd up` changes the allowlist, it rolls Dex so it serves the new one; users then sign in
again, since a token they hold keeps its old teams until it expires.

IMPORTANT: removing or renaming a namespace entry deletes that namespace and every workspace and
volume in it. `jd config` refuses to remove a namespace that still holds a workspace, more than 5
namespaces in one change, or a template a live workspace still uses. Removing a team from a
namespace with workspaces only warns: the workspaces stay, but their owners in that team lose access.
Never work around these guards by deleting workspaces on the user's behalf.

The OAuth app itself must be registered with the GitHub organization that owns these teams.
When creating the OAuth app, log in as a member of the organization and set the
`Authorization callback URL` to `https://<subdomain>.<domain>/dex/callback`. Organization
owners may need to approve the OAuth app for it to access team membership; see GitHub's
[third-party access settings](https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-oauth-access-to-your-organizations-data).

