### Coding agents (Claude Code, Codex) on Amazon Bedrock

The JupyterLab image ships `claude` and `codex`. With `workspace_bedrock_enabled: true` (the
default), workspaces get AWS credentials through EKS Pod Identity and both CLIs call Amazon
Bedrock with no setup. The variables:
- `workspace_bedrock_enabled` turns the credentials on for every workspace namespace; an entry of
  `workspace_namespaces` opts its namespace out with `bedrock: "false"`
- `workspace_bedrock_model_patterns` lists the models workspaces may invoke (IAM wildcards)
- `workspace_bedrock_inference_scope` is `geo` (requests stay in the region's geography) or `global`;
  Codex has no geographic profile outside the US, so it needs `global` there
- `workspace_app_claude_code_version` and `workspace_app_codex_version` pin the CLIs in the image;
  each CLI's default model follows its version (Codex's is the one its release recommends for the
  inference profile prefix, set at image build time)

Verify from a workspace, headless (no first-run dialogs):

```bash
jd server exec --name <workspace-name> --scope <namespace> -- "claude -p 'Reply with OK'"
jd server exec --name <workspace-name> --scope <namespace> -- "codex exec --skip-git-repo-check 'Reply with OK'"
```

IMPORTANT: in an account that never used a model, the first invocation enables it, through an AWS
Marketplace subscription that the workspace roles cannot start, and accepts the model provider's
EULA. Anthropic models also need a one-time first-time-use form with the deployer's company details.
These are the deployer's decisions: point the user to TROUBLESHOOT.md, and never invoke a model,
accept an agreement or submit the form on their behalf.

Changes reach a workspace on its next start.

### Workspace volume snapshots

With `workspace_volume_snapshots_enabled: true` (the default), an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager
(DLM) policy snapshots each workspace home volume every `workspace_volume_snapshot_interval_hours`
and keeps the latest `workspace_volume_snapshot_retain_count` snapshots, so the user can restore a
deleted workspace or namespace from them. List them with
`aws ec2 describe-snapshots --owner-ids self --filters Name=tag:DeploymentId,Values=$(jd show -o deployment_id --text)`.
IMPORTANT: `jd down` deletes every snapshot tagged with the deployment's `DeploymentId`; warn the
user before a teardown if they may still need one. To keep a snapshot, copy it: the copy carries no
tags, so neither `jd down` nor the DLM retention rule deletes it.

```bash
aws ec2 copy-snapshot --source-region <region> --source-snapshot-id <snapshot-id> --description "<why>"
```

Removing the `DeploymentId` tag (`aws ec2 delete-tags --resources <snapshot-id> --tags Key=DeploymentId`)
also spares it from `jd down`, but DLM's retention rule still deletes it until the policy is gone, so
only do it right before the teardown.

